WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.920
Commando, can Super Joe and you carry out this reckless, almost impossible mission?

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:28.280
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.100
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:31.180
And I'm Joe.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:37.140
Guys, the name of the game today, Commando, another one of those Captain Commando Super Series

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.460
games for the NES that Capcom made.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.000
Capcom. Capcom, Commando.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:42.880
Captain Commando.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:44.340
Yeah, Captain Commando.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:45.880
I know we tried to make that last time.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:47.220
We'll make it a thing.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:48.740
It was like a reach. Conspiracy theory.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.680
I liked it. I actually thought it worked.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:56.780
Guys, Commando, any initial impressions, first impressions?

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:58.620
You guys want to just jump right into it?

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:03.860
Yeah. Um, I, I think that this is a okay game.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.640
I think that it has some good value and just like, you know, I like the, I like the, um,

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:11.740
I like the setting.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:14.800
I like the helicopter entry.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.000
The cut scenes sold you on the game.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.420
The cut scenes are so good. The helicopter with one blade.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:26.860
Yeah. But I found the gameplay itself has aged poorly and just going up one generation and making

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:31.220
this what it could have been on the Super NES would make this a much better game.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.020
All right, Joe.

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:37.600
Joe? Yeah, this is one of the first games that, uh, you know, I, I feel like we're going to

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.260
see a lot of games like this on the NES.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.220
Like it's something that like this kind of run and gun game is something that I think of

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:44.140
when I think about the NES.

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:47.000
Um, and I thought it, I thought it was a fun game.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:50.460
I can't disagree that it, that it hasn't aged super well.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:56.300
Um, but that being said, it was, I think the difficulty level was what my first impression

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:59.160
was, was it wasn't so difficult that it was annoying.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:02.240
It was, it was, it was a fair and like fun level of difficulty.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:08.060
I thought. I'm always delighted when we have a playable game, you know, uh, with these third party

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:12.120
thing, Capcom has really been doing a great job of making sure that their games are like

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:18.120
good and playable. And I think that's how they, by starting so early on and getting these good games on board,

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:23.320
I think a lot of, uh, you know, kids and fans of games were able to like trust them a little

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:28.320
more than the average developer because like these were big arcade releases to a commando

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.320
and coming up next ghost and goblins.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:35.560
Like both of those games were like huge in the arcade and their NES ports are good, but

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:41.260
I do feel like, you know, my, my thing is, is that the NES is like limiting the possibilities

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:42.220
for this game.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:47.920
I mean, right off the bat, like the, um, the sprite limitation is, is huge.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:51.240
And like, and you know, like the whole point is, is like, you know, I got to shoot that

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:54.040
guy. I should shoot that guy. It's like, not if they disappear before you.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:58.960
And so that's just like, that's a weird thing about this game that definitely doesn't happen

00:02:58.960 --> 00:02:59.940
in the arcade version.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.980
But you know, we've talked about how the arcade versions are always better.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:06.720
I mean, 1942 had a much better arcade version.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.880
It's just, you know, it's just something that like, obviously the NES couldn't replicate the

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:16.020
arcade hardware. So I'm not saying that so much as a complaint, just like a first initial impression is that like

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:19.560
this game doesn't really like hold up as well as it does on arcade.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:25.120
And if I can expand on that a little bit, I just, I'm just questioning why the decision

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:30.860
was made to go with maybe as many sprites on the screen for each enemy that you're, that

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:32.140
you're fighting against in this game.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:37.340
As you scroll up the screen, um, why would they allow as many enemies to be on the screen

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:43.100
if it was actually going to cause issues such as, um, extreme flicker and actual sprite

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:44.460
disappearance at times?

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:49.520
I'm wondering if it's just more like a lazy port of just like being like, Hey, let's just

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:52.800
take the game code that we have and, you know, mix it over.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:56.780
Cause it's probably done in something similar to assembly, which is what the NES is coded

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:00.140
on and just being like, Hey, let's just take this over and it works.

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:02.060
Oh great. It works. We don't have to change anything.

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:07.460
Like, you know, it, that's not like probably not what actually happened, but I think that's

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:09.660
a good enough explanation of like why they're okay with this.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:11.080
I mean, flickering is nothing new.

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:14.100
Everybody remembers how hard everything flickered in 10 yard fight.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:15.560
Oh, the first game we played.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:19.680
The thing with, I think with 10 yard fight, the sprites are so much smaller that it doesn't

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.020
like stand out. The flickering doesn't stand out as much.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:23.440
And the linebackers didn't disappear.

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:24.460
No one disappears.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:28.520
Which is important because you want to make sure that all your players are, you know,

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:33.020
physical appearances and able to stop the people and that goes through them.

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:36.220
Should we give a quick like rundown of what's going on in the game?

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.620
Yeah. I'd love to. Joe, would you like to tackle that?

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:42.780
Sure. So yeah, you play as a lone commando.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:44.020
Do you know his name?

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:46.320
Joe. Only from your intro.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:48.320
I didn't know that. His name is Super Joe.

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:50.000
And I thought that was funny. I was like, well, you know.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:54.920
So you play as Super Joe dropped into this war zone alone, completely alone with just,

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:58.720
I mean, dozens and dozens, hundreds maybe of enemies.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:00.360
And it seems like you have an army, right?

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:03.820
I mean, like a helicopter drops you off and it's like, okay, this is all you now.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:07.420
I like to think that you're just late because there are guys that are here.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:08.460
They've just been captured.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:11.080
I think we lost the war and you just got there.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:12.880
Well, yeah, you, you.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:18.460
This is, this is like when, um, you know, like Donald Trump finally after his foot, it's

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:20.560
like, you know, it's like, okay, I'm ready for the war now.

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:23.520
And he goes in there and it's like, well, I mean, there's nothing really to do here.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:24.680
Sorry. They captured everybody.

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:27.540
You basically just run forward.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:29.820
I mean, you run forward and you shoot, you shoot at enemies.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:31.300
Bullets come at you nice and slowly.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:32.360
You can dodge out of the way.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:34.760
You just bring up a really good point though for a second.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:36.100
Did anybody ever try to go backwards?

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:37.560
Can you? Does it lock on you?

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:38.880
You cannot. Okay. I never tried.

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:42.280
It's such a forward thinking game of just like move forward.

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:45.400
Sometimes I forgot that I, that I was controlling it going forward.

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:47.120
I was like, I can stop if I want.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:48.060
I kept forgetting.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:50.840
This is literally just like a hand cranked shmup.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:53.180
Right. I like that actually.

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:56.620
It would actually be weird if this game was on rails.

00:05:56.840 --> 00:06:02.020
Yeah. But it would add a bit of challenge of you like having to like avoid obstacles before being

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:05.900
crushed by the screen, which doesn't make any sense when you get crushed by a screen.

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:08.300
It's like, I know that there was things behind me.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:12.400
Like I'm, I'm in an empty space just cause the camera's rolling, but that's, that's beyond

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:21.560
the point. I was playing with Chris, our audio producer who's in the room and, uh, and there was a point,

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:26.100
there was a point when I actually said, not jokingly, I was like, watch out for that bullet

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:29.720
because like, even it was so, the bullets come so slow at you.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:32.220
You have time to be like, look out for that bullet and get out of the way.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:37.040
I didn't realize until after I said it, how, uh, yeah, I guess I never really thought about

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:41.880
that. But, uh, Joe, you brought up that, you know, the game's kind of easy and I, I agree.

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:46.300
I think that I did like a full pacifist run on this game.

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:50.900
Uh, the first or not the first time I beat it, essentially, I was just like, Hmm, seems

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:53.260
like all I have to really do is just dodge everyone's bullets.

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:54.680
And it's, it's pretty easy.

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:58.460
And I didn't, you don't have to attack anybody until the very end of the stage.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:03.140
But when you say that you beat it, do you mean you beat all four levels or?

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:09.180
Okay. So I beat four levels, but, but they let you keep looping over the same four levels.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:10.800
Until you get to four, four.

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:13.740
So it's like, there's, it's, it's really lazy.

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:17.480
It's there's one, four, which is its own stage, two, four, three, four, and four, four.

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:18.780
All of those are their own stages.

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:23.380
Two, one, two, two, two, three, two, four are all the same stages again.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:25.460
No differences. No, there's differences.

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:27.860
The difficulty wraps up.

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:28.860
Yeah. That's it. The difficulty.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:30.980
That's okay. The map itself is the same though.

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:32.220
Right. The map is exactly the same.

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:34.120
Yeah. Like what the enemy is and stuff like that.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:39.080
So I think like that's not enough to warrant me playing through, uh, you know, like I

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:39.940
can't even, what's the math?

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:41.400
32, 32 levels.

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:43.260
Can't even do like, right.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:44.460
Actually, I don't know. 16.

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:45.800
Yeah. I think it's 16.

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:48.280
All right. I wish there was 32 levels.

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:52.160
I mean, 32 different levels, but yeah, I don't think like there was enough there.

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:56.200
So I'm going to say beating the game is just getting to one four, but like, yeah, I agree,

00:07:56.260 --> 00:07:59.660
but I just wanted to say that because I'm sure there's someone out there that's beaten

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:04.400
the whole game. That's like, well, you guys are saying it's easy and like maybe, maybe the fourth time around

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:05.640
is really hard. I don't know.

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:07.420
Cause I only played the first, the first time around.

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:11.420
Yeah. I just think like, it just says something about the game though, that I didn't even

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:15.520
have to, you know, do anything other than like up and to the right and left.

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:20.460
I was doing like 10 yard fight style zigzagging through the course and people just couldn't

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:24.000
like the AI just couldn't figure out where I was going to be at any given time.

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:29.140
I guess that's just a difference of like the philosophy of how you want to play because

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:32.360
I didn't even think to avoid guys.

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:36.820
I mean, I was trying to kill them all and I think that that's what a lot, most of the

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:38.600
players would be trying to do.

00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:43.500
Just because this is one of those score based games, the typical arcade.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:51.600
Um, so I understand that you can get through the level without, um, without killing anyone

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:52.560
until the end.

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:57.140
I guess that's the same as like, I guess in guitar hero, you could skip entire sections

00:08:57.140 --> 00:08:58.360
if it was too hard.

00:08:58.520 --> 00:08:59.820
It's funny that you bring that up.

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:04.220
There was actually like a guy who had like a pretty weird username, uh, that I don't remember,

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:06.620
but he was on score hero, which is like a guitar hero website.

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:10.140
Right. And he would purposely try to get the lowest score.

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:14.580
Like the minimum, like what is the lowest score you can get without failing the song?

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:19.560
And so I guess it's in a way what I was trying to do with this game was just like, do I have

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:23.880
to kill anybody? Like even the prisoners of war, it's like, all you have to do is just tag them.

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:25.680
You know, you don't have to like, you're in buddy.

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:32.800
Like, yeah, you're in. And speaking of the prisoners of war, I just think like they're inconsistent in like how

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:37.720
they work. Like sometimes you'll tap them and they'll like flash like a thousand points.

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:39.560
And other times they'll just straight up disappear.

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:41.220
And I'm like, did I get the points?

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:42.320
Like, did I do something wrong?

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:43.280
Did someone shoot them?

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:44.380
And like, what happened?

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:48.020
I think that they just got taken out by a memory issue.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:49.400
A memory issue took them out.

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:50.340
It's like, rest in peace.

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:54.500
Because sometimes they'll be, they're, they're escorted by, you know, by soldiers that are

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:55.840
taking them, I guess, to prison.

00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:59.980
And then you shoot them, you get the points, but sometimes you shoot the, you shoot the,

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:01.860
not the prisoners of war, you shoot the guards.

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:06.180
Um, but sometimes you shoot the guards and the prisoner just keeps going, like dutifully

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:08.840
marching towards his prison without anyone like taking him there.

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:10.660
Stockholm syndrome. Yeah, exactly.

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:12.320
It's his mission.

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:16.260
And also some of the prisoners of war, uh, look like they're like about to pee, you know,

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:17.500
like, like, or they're taking a pee.

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:21.560
They got a real like potty dance, uh, like, uh, animation going.

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:25.760
Yeah. I'm just not sure what that's about, but you know, uh, animation, like, you know, we're

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:29.020
watching it right now and there's two guys and they're next to trees too.

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:33.020
So that would be like an appropriate spot to go to the bathroom, but bullets are flying

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:35.320
everywhere. And it's like, what is this?

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:36.360
Like, what are you doing right now?

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:37.340
Nature calls, man.

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:38.340
It's a sick joke.

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:40.900
Uh, the guy, I'm the commando super Joe.

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:42.540
Who's just running through the course.

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:53.700
All right. So, I mean, I only went through, uh, you know, up to one four, but did, I mean, like what's

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:57.200
weird about it is it's like, you know, you're on your way to the, like it's building up this

00:10:57.200 --> 00:10:58.380
whole thing to one four.

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:02.260
It doesn't really like encourage you to play to one.

00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:05.860
It's not like, you know, it's just like saying the same exact things as it was saying in the

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:09.240
first round. So it doesn't even feel like there's enough story here.

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:14.460
But it does say at the, at the end of one four, first of all, it says, congratulations

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:15.900
on your first mission.

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:17.280
This is your first mission.

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:21.940
They dropped you in there alone, alone in an entire army of your enemy.

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:28.420
Um, and also in between each level, there's like this, this like rugged, I don't know what

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:29.540
like lieutenant or something.

00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:31.200
It's always like smoking a cigar or something.

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:32.680
And he's like, that's you.

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:34.640
I always thought he was giving you a command.

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:35.880
Oh, I don't know.

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:38.860
No, I thought it was someone because it always say now go on to your next one.

