But other than that, no, I think it's just a very basic thing.

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:10.700
And then there's those cars in the second area, area one, two, I guess.

00:13:10.900 --> 00:13:16.040
Um, and they just like, it seems like no matter what you do sometimes, unless you back up,

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:17.060
they're going to run you over.

00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:20.840
Like you can't, you can't like go in and around them or anything like that.

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:22.760
Like they just appear out of nowhere.

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:25.700
And it's like, if you don't back up, you're about to get hit by those cars.

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:29.840
And I just, I felt like that was a very cheap, weird thing to do in a game that otherwise

00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:30.920
is just like bullet.

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:32.400
That's in two one, right?

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:33.840
Well, I, no, no, no.

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:35.040
It's the street level.

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:37.120
Oh, okay. It's like either one, two or one, three.

00:13:37.340 --> 00:13:42.520
Cause in, in one, one there's cars or maybe in, in one, two there's cars, but you can dodge

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:46.140
them. But then the reason I realized that it was harder the second time around, cause it's like

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:50.340
as far as I got, cause when the second time around the cars just like follow you, you

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:54.780
have, you have to back up to the point where they just decide to back up all the way off

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:58.360
the screen. Like they only go to a certain port and then back all the way back off the screen.

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:01.840
Well, because it seems like everybody is like afraid of the screen, you know, everybody's

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:02.880
afraid of that camera.

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:07.700
Also at that point after your first mission, you've killed hundreds of men in, in one go

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:09.100
with like no, with no backup.

00:14:09.100 --> 00:14:10.720
So like they're scared of you, I'm sure.

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:15.020
And then as far as your weapons go, I mean, you're mostly just using the machine gun to

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:19.040
shoot, but has anybody, has anybody like use the grenades?

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:24.460
I did use the grenades because they were the only way to actually know where I was going

00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:32.020
to shoot. Like one of the reasons that I thought that this game was ill suited for the NES is because

00:14:32.020 --> 00:14:35.000
you are always shooting in the direction that you're walking.

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:41.480
And so if you are trying to get around a guy that's kind of following you, you have to do

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:45.100
these weird maneuvers where you like, oh, I have to walk away for a certain amount.

00:14:45.220 --> 00:14:49.840
And then I'll, I'll turn around for a split second and shoot and walk away again so that

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:52.340
I can dodge these guys at the same time as killing them.

00:14:53.040 --> 00:15:02.120
I think that if this had a, not, not a twin stick, but maybe a super smash sort of controller

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:07.600
scheme so that you could shoot in the directions of all the deep, the, the, uh, the deep, the

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:08.700
directional buttons, I'm sorry.

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:13.100
Um, I think that would make this a better feeling game.

00:15:13.380 --> 00:15:15.440
Uh, and maybe it would make it a little easier.

00:15:15.940 --> 00:15:18.240
Um, and maybe they didn't want to go that route.

00:15:18.420 --> 00:15:22.400
It might also look a little crazy if you're able to like walk forward at the same time

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:25.900
that you're shooting backwards or something like, yeah, it would probably, maybe it would

00:15:25.900 --> 00:15:28.680
look weird, but I think that it would, but you're the commando.

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:29.960
You're super, you're crazy.

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:33.320
You literally entered a battlefield all on your own and said, screw it.

00:15:33.400 --> 00:15:36.160
I'm going for it. So I guess that does make a little bit of sense.

00:15:36.260 --> 00:15:38.200
And that was one thing that I noticed too.

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:42.580
And I noticed that, you know, that's, it wasn't designed perfectly gameplay wise, but

00:15:42.580 --> 00:15:46.600
like, I thought that kind of like accidentally, accidentally ended up being sort of fun where

00:15:46.600 --> 00:15:49.300
it's like, I got to dodge the bullets and turn around, you know, it's like obviously

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:50.140
kind of stupid.

00:15:50.520 --> 00:15:52.200
I want to play games with one hand, you know?

00:15:52.540 --> 00:15:56.740
I think that's also like what influenced my pacifist run is just, I was like, well, my real

00:15:56.740 --> 00:15:58.200
mission is just to get to the end of the level.

00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:00.600
So I should just try to get there as fast as possible.

00:16:00.800 --> 00:16:05.720
Like, you know, it doesn't make sense if I'm going like sideways and backwards to shoot

00:16:05.720 --> 00:16:09.320
down these enemies because like who would stop in a real war and be like, oh, I didn't kill

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:10.660
that guy. Let me go back. Boom, boom, boom.

00:16:10.780 --> 00:16:12.560
Like, you know, it's like, you would probably just, I don't know.

00:16:12.660 --> 00:16:15.500
Maybe, I mean, I guess it kind of depends on what your mission is.

00:16:15.620 --> 00:16:18.740
Well, when the bullets are going that slow though too, it's like, you know, in real time,

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:22.500
it's like, you want to make sure you get everybody because you know, the bullets only, you don't

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:23.780
have that much time to react.

00:16:24.460 --> 00:16:26.220
Now we're talking about the philosophy of war.

00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:28.120
Right. And that's a whole nother episode.

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:30.800
I'm sure people aren't going to stick around for.

00:16:32.020 --> 00:16:35.160
What was the last thing I wanted to talk about with Commando?

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:36.780
We had the grenades, we had the cars.

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:39.160
There was no like boss battle.

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:41.100
Isn't that kind of weird? That is strange.

00:16:41.320 --> 00:16:42.300
But the boss.

00:16:42.460 --> 00:16:44.240
I thought the boss should have been like a helicopter.

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:48.820
The end of each level is like, it's not a boss, but it's like a boss event.

00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:51.720
Like you run into a wall, the wall is closed.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:53.020
There's a ton of enemies that come at you.

00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:56.940
You have nowhere to go. So you have to kill every one of the enemies, including people with riot shields.

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:58.500
So it's like not one boss.

00:16:58.740 --> 00:17:02.020
It's a crescendo. I just would like, I think Super Joe needs a super foe.

00:17:02.280 --> 00:17:03.700
Oh my. Maybe.

00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:05.420
I wonder if there's a boss at the very end.

00:17:05.960 --> 00:17:07.060
A 4-4? Yeah.

00:17:07.080 --> 00:17:08.220
I can guarantee you there isn't.

00:17:08.600 --> 00:17:09.820
That's fair. I'm willing to guarantee that.

00:17:09.820 --> 00:17:10.880
Is the helicopter about to go?

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:14.480
Oh, never mind. Well, see, that's what I was saying is that I think it would be really cool if the helicopter

00:17:14.480 --> 00:17:15.700
DM was the boss.

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:19.420
It was a twist. Whether it's your helicopter or not, I don't care.

00:17:19.460 --> 00:17:24.200
But like, I do think it's funny that at the end of the mission of 1-4, it's like the

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:28.420
helicopter just comes, picks you up, and just drops you off like maybe like what would

00:17:28.420 --> 00:17:31.000
have taken you 10 seconds to walk forward to.

00:17:31.180 --> 00:17:32.620
And it's like, here you go, man.

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:34.160
Like as far as I can take you.

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:35.520
And it's like, wait, what do you mean?

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:40.460
And also it doesn't even like gradually change from like the grass area to the desert area.

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:41.980
It just pops back. It's all of a sudden.

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:43.580
Yeah, yeah, yeah. It's like you've entered.

00:17:43.580 --> 00:17:50.180
It reminds me of like areas in early N64 and PS1 games where it would be like once you've

00:17:50.180 --> 00:17:51.900
entered that zone, it's like, it's snowing here.

00:17:52.340 --> 00:17:57.460
Right. It's like, okay, but the second I step outside that mark, it's like it's not snowing anymore.

00:18:04.180 --> 00:18:04.980
All right.

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:09.440
So we've talked at length about Commando, I think, about as much as we can talk about that.

00:18:09.440 --> 00:18:12.600
But I would like to talk about the director of the game.

00:18:12.760 --> 00:18:16.880
And I'm going to botch this one, but I think it's Tokuro Fujiwara.

00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:25.080
And he is the director of Commando, Ghosts and Goblins, and then a big NES title, Mega Man.

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:27.180
That's a pretty good lineup.

00:18:27.180 --> 00:18:31.200
But he's still like working with Capcom to this day.

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:37.200
And I think that that's an interesting like feat for him that he started off all these, you know, like old games.

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:44.960
And he's still working with the company more as like a I think he's like the general manager of Capcom or he was the general manager of Capcom.

00:18:44.980 --> 00:18:46.820
And I think he's just more of like a supervisor role.

00:18:46.820 --> 00:18:51.940
But he also conceived the idea for Resident Evil, which is which is weird.

00:18:52.180 --> 00:18:55.060
And you just told me that right before we started recording.

00:18:55.240 --> 00:19:02.340
I am amazed that like that he's sort of still a well, I mean, yeah, you just said that he wasn't doing as much anymore.

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:04.460
And just kind of like a consultant now.

00:19:04.660 --> 00:19:08.220
But I it's it shows how far the guy came.

00:19:08.460 --> 00:19:13.200
Yeah. Coming from something that was kind of just a rehash of of Commando.

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:19.980
Commando or it's a lot of well, it's like a lot of different stuff, though, too, you know, like Commando, Ghosts and Goblins, Mega Man.

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:24.220
I mean, they all have like that either like action or platformy aspect to them.

00:19:24.280 --> 00:19:27.700
But they're all like incredibly different types of games, even in their genre.

00:19:27.920 --> 00:19:30.320
And most of them have a legacy, I think, except for Commando.

00:19:30.500 --> 00:19:31.520
Right. And Resident Evil.

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:35.800
Well, Commando does get a bit of a legacy in Bionic Commando, which is like very.

00:19:36.060 --> 00:19:42.740
Oh, it's a spiritual successor. Well, yeah, it's like spiritual successor, definitely made by Capcom and like loosely tied to Commando in some ways.

00:19:42.740 --> 00:19:46.460
Super Joe winds up making an appearance in some of the Bionic Commando games.

00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:47.340
You don't play as him.

00:19:47.540 --> 00:19:48.920
I think that's like a neat little thing.

00:19:49.240 --> 00:19:55.160
But, you know, to then jump over to like Resident Evil, I think that's pretty cool for Fujiwara to like be such an imaginative guy.

00:19:55.440 --> 00:20:02.920
And Resident Evil comes from the idea that he he made an original game called Sweet Home, which I'd love to like watch a video of.

00:20:03.020 --> 00:20:04.960
I haven't watched it at all if it is playable.

00:20:05.120 --> 00:20:10.560
But he made this game called Sweet Home and then was like, why don't we make this Resident Evil like a full length game?

00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:12.760
Oh, Sweet Home, was that like a haunted house sort of thing?

00:20:12.760 --> 00:20:14.340
Sounds like a Silent Hill kind of thing, right?

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:15.880
I think that'd be kind of interesting to see.

00:20:16.060 --> 00:20:17.200
Whoa, don't don't don't compare the two.

00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:18.460
Well, yeah, I'm sorry.

00:20:18.700 --> 00:20:19.680
There is a big difference.

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:26.860
But Sweet Home doesn't sound like it would be, you know, as big of a scale as Resident Evil is and would be more about like.

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:28.820
It would be more intimate, I would say.

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:29.920
Right, right. That's what I would think.

00:20:30.100 --> 00:20:41.920
Yeah, yeah. Like, I can't run a good example for this game now, but there's plenty of indie games currently out that are trying to like replicate that feeling of like, you're in a house and something spooky is happening, you know, kind of like what Resident Evil 7 did a little bit.

00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:44.760
Yeah, I would say like that, like kind of outlast maybe.

00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:50.080
Right. We're all we're kind of we're jumping on a huge tangent here, but we're staying relevant by talking.

00:20:50.380 --> 00:20:54.360
No, I'm saying that we're making a lot of speculation about a game we've never played.

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:56.160
Right. I would actually love to see the game now.

00:20:56.160 --> 00:21:02.860
And it's actually just like some 16 bit horror game in the same way that like Ghost and Goblins is a horror game.

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:05.720
And then one more thing.

00:21:06.200 --> 00:21:11.200
Shinji Mikami, who is the director of Resident Evil 1 and Dino Crisis.

00:21:11.700 --> 00:21:14.240
Oh, my God. I think there is a following for that game.

00:21:14.480 --> 00:21:15.560
I like Dino Crisis.

00:21:16.440 --> 00:21:23.520
He was he was asked about Fujiwara just like as like a, you know, like, who do you respect in your company and stuff like that?

00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:30.260
And he immediately answered Fujiwara and then was quoted as far as saying, yeah, he is a scary master for me.

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:31.940
Maybe evil master.

00:21:32.200 --> 00:21:34.020
I still can't compete with him.

00:21:34.220 --> 00:21:35.920
I'll put myself at his feet.

00:21:36.140 --> 00:21:39.520
He has some kind of different atmosphere than other people.

00:21:39.760 --> 00:21:45.400
He is not big or macho and he doesn't raise his voice either, but he is really scary.

00:21:45.680 --> 00:21:47.940
His way is not yes or no.

00:21:48.260 --> 00:21:49.560
Only yes.

00:21:49.860 --> 00:21:51.480
I learned a lot from him.

00:21:51.480 --> 00:21:56.260
One big thing I learned from him is create freedom inside restriction.

00:21:57.300 --> 00:22:00.560
Interesting. Yeah. Mikami sounds like he learned a lot from Fujiwara.

00:22:00.660 --> 00:22:03.600
And I think I just learned something to freedom inside restriction.

00:22:04.080 --> 00:22:09.220
I like and I like the idea that maybe, you know, I mean, like we're seeing commando here and it's like that's that's not necessarily true.

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:12.900
He had a restriction and then like the sprite limit and they were like, go for it.

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:19.100
But then again, that's really that's like a bad representation of Fujiwara because I'm sure he only worked on the arcade version.

00:22:19.500 --> 00:22:21.520
Yeah. And never like was responsible.

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:26.700
Similar like how Miyamoto wasn't like watching the Donkey Kong port and being like, you know what?

00:22:26.760 --> 00:22:28.380
Let's leave out that third level.

00:22:28.640 --> 00:22:30.400
Like, I don't want that in my game.

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:33.140
Yeah. So, I mean, it's pretty much Fujiwara in a nutshell.

00:22:33.140 --> 00:22:57.960
But I did think one thing that was cool about commando and him is that if you are if you go to round four, four, which is the final round of the game and you have three lives and 24 grenades and beat the game, you get a special message at the end of the game that it says something like, you know, my next game is Captain Higemaru.

00:22:58.420 --> 00:22:59.260
Please play it.

00:22:59.320 --> 00:23:03.000
You know, you know.

00:23:03.140 --> 00:23:05.020
I didn't know. Like, it really says, you know.

00:23:05.300 --> 00:23:09.020
And like, I think that's really cool because that actually did wind up being his next game.

00:23:09.140 --> 00:23:11.100
But it's like it's a pretty random restriction.

00:23:11.100 --> 00:23:15.440
And this is like all that pre-internet stuff where like people would just say they saw that one time.

00:23:15.540 --> 00:23:17.920
It's not even a hard restriction if you think about it.

00:23:17.960 --> 00:23:19.320
Like, you just have to have the three lives.

00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:22.640
It sounds hard to me. Do you have to have at least 24 grenades?

00:23:22.860 --> 00:23:24.500
No, no, no. It's exactly those numbers.

00:23:24.680 --> 00:23:31.160
It's coded in the game as if player equals three and hand bomb equals 24, display this message.

00:23:31.600 --> 00:23:33.660
And Captain Higemaru was his next game.

00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:36.420
And I don't think that ever came to North America NES.

00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:38.640
But it's like a pirate puzzle game.

00:23:38.780 --> 00:23:40.680
So I'm sure people are going to go look that up now.

00:23:40.740 --> 00:23:42.940
It's even worse than that message is in there.

00:23:43.320 --> 00:23:45.200
Yeah. Because then you're like, yeah, I got this message.

00:23:45.280 --> 00:23:46.300
Take it out. It never comes out.

00:23:46.300 --> 00:23:53.200
But I think like this would have also probably played in the arcade too, which, you know, Captain Higemaru would have been on as well.

00:23:53.420 --> 00:23:55.340
So it's just like leftover remnants.

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:59.020
Which leads me to believe our thought earlier about this just being a lazy port.

00:23:59.180 --> 00:24:00.360
Yeah, an extreme port.

00:24:00.360 --> 00:24:07.700
I will say that if you play this on an emulator, just take off the or rather put on like sprite limiters.

00:24:07.820 --> 00:24:13.760
This way it like allows like an infinite number of sprites to be displayed and not the like whatever your CPU can handle.

00:24:14.160 --> 00:24:17.360
And then you'll have a great experience because nobody will flicker.

00:24:17.580 --> 00:24:18.980
All the enemies will be on the screen.

00:24:19.160 --> 00:24:20.340
It's definitely a little more challenging.

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:22.160
I like seeing it in that respect.

00:24:22.340 --> 00:24:25.220
But, you know, that's not really the way the game was intended to be played.

00:24:25.340 --> 00:24:27.520
That's a legal mic. Well, I'm saying please play it both ways.

00:24:27.520 --> 00:24:28.960
Well, we talked about that earlier too, though.

00:24:29.060 --> 00:24:32.100
I did the frame limiter on 1942.

00:24:32.540 --> 00:24:35.600
Really? To make it like, I had like 10 frame limits.

00:24:35.900 --> 00:24:43.500
So this way it would skip every like 10 frames or so to make it look like 60 FPS smooth gameplay, even though it wasn't.

00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:49.480
And sometimes there would be like some weird glitches where I would like hit an enemy, but it wouldn't destroy my ship because it didn't register as one of the frames.

00:24:49.680 --> 00:24:51.440
So we hadn't talked about that before?

00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:53.060
We did. We talked about it in 1942.

00:24:53.440 --> 00:24:55.080
I had a vague recollection of that.

00:24:55.080 --> 00:25:00.620
It's like an interesting way of, I mean, like it wouldn't work in this game for sure because you dodge so many bullets by skipping those frames.

00:25:00.980 --> 00:25:05.560
But I think like it's an interesting way to see like games play a lot smoother than they do.

00:25:05.920 --> 00:25:11.320
Because technically every NES game plays at 60 FPS, but it definitely doesn't feel like it.

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:14.440
There's definitely laggy sections.

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:16.220
Joe, you had some straight thoughts?

00:25:16.220 --> 00:25:24.060
I did, which I just added another straight thought in the middle of that because we're watching footage of just random footage of the game.

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:27.760
And while none of us in the room beat the game in the footage, we get to the end of the game.

00:25:27.780 --> 00:25:33.460
And now I'm just going off of, I just saw for a second here, but it says something like your game is ended.

00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:36.060
You're a great player at the end.

00:25:36.060 --> 00:25:38.500
If only every game could...

00:25:38.500 --> 00:25:39.480
You can speak.

00:25:39.860 --> 00:25:41.900
You're. Yeah, it's been...

00:25:41.900 --> 00:25:43.720
Y-O-U-R. Yeah, Y-O-U-R.

00:25:43.720 --> 00:25:44.540
Y-O-U-R.

00:25:44.660 --> 00:25:45.900
The incorrect you're.

00:25:45.900 --> 00:25:47.480
That is so frequent.

00:25:47.900 --> 00:25:50.800
In every NES game, you're never going to get the proper grammar.

00:25:52.460 --> 00:25:55.160
But my other straight thoughts that I had before this was...

00:25:55.160 --> 00:25:56.740
Wait, just one thing though about that one though.

00:25:56.900 --> 00:26:02.080
I do, I always appreciate it when an end game like commends you on being a good player.

00:26:02.080 --> 00:26:06.680
Because that's really all these games were was like, there wasn't like a giant story that needed closure.

00:26:06.860 --> 00:26:08.880
It was more like, holy shoot, you did it.

00:26:09.060 --> 00:26:10.040
You know, like that's incredible.

00:26:10.420 --> 00:26:12.760
Here's a pat on the back. Yeah, you're really good at video games.

00:26:12.880 --> 00:26:14.940
Like that's the kind of stuff that you wanted to see as a kid.

00:26:15.020 --> 00:26:16.460
So I appreciate messages like that.

00:26:16.580 --> 00:26:18.940
It's much better than just like, the end.

00:26:20.360 --> 00:26:21.840
Later that same day.

